{"edits":[{"content":"The objection that SRC_001's non-compositionality proof is flawed because it posits 'overlapping attacks' across disjoint argument sets misunderstands the intended meaning. In abstract argumentation, 'overlapping' need not imply that an attack crosses from one framework to another; rather, it can denote a structural correspondence between attack relations. For instance, if F1 has an attack (a1,b1) and F2 has an attack (a2,b2) such that a1↔a2 and b1↔b2 under an isomorphism, these attacks are 'overlapping' in the relevant sense. When F1 and F2 are combined, the grounded extension of the union is not necessarily the union of the grounded extensions, because the fixed-point computation over the entire attack set can yield a different outcome. Thus, the original claim—that modular expansion must account for inter-framework interactions or shared structure—stands. The critic's alternative explanation (inter-framework attacks) does not refute this; it merely offers a different lens that is compatible with the target's conclusion."},{"content":"The critique that SRC_001 relies on a contradictory notion of 'overlapping attacks' between disjoint argument sets is based on a false premise. In the standard framework, attacks are pairs (source, target) within a single set. Two disjoint frameworks can exhibit overlapping attacks if their attack relations are isomorphic—i.e., the same pattern appears in both, such as (a1,b1) in F1 and (a2,b2) in F2 with a1~a2 and b1~b2. The non-compositionality phenomenon is not about attacks crossing boundaries but about the failure of the grounded extension to distribute over union. Specifically, the grounded extension of F1∪F2 may differ from the union of the grounded extensions of F1 and F2 because the least fixed point of the combined argumentation framework depends on the global attack structure. Therefore, the target's assertion is well-defined and its implication about modular expansion is valid. The critic has not shown a flaw; they have only proposed an alternative characterization that does not invalidate the original reasoning."}]}